---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { originOf } from "../lib/archive.ts";

const site = originOf(Astro.site);
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  "@id": `${site}/what`,
  url: `${site}/what`,
  name: "What",
  isPartOf: { "@id": `${site}/#site` },
};
---

<Base
  title="What — latlon.art"
  description="What a transient is, in theory and in practice."
  robots="noindex"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><a href="/">latlon.art</a></p>
  <h1>What</h1>
  <p class="line">Transient: the most precise possible measurement of someone (or something) in space-time.</p>
  <p class="prose">
    In theory, a transient is every possible measurement of someone or something at one point in
    spacetime: what any sensor could read, its physical makeup down to the count of its atoms, its
    time, and its quantum, chemical and biological state. That set can never be fully measured. A
    transient is only fully valid in the exact spacetime where it was made, so it stops being valid
    the moment it is finished, because time moves forward. What is left is the space, plus any other
    measurements that can be repeated here and now at that spot. In practice, a transient records what
    can be measured: latitude, longitude, altitude and UTC time, written on a physical object, such as
    a sign or a tag, and left at that spot. A transient is at home, and most authentic, where its
    measurements still match its surroundings. Moved somewhere else, it still points back to where it
    was made.
  </p>
  <p class="quiet apart"><a href="/">The transients.</a></p>
</Base>
